entry nuclear-stability
term: Nuclear Stability
related_terms: nucleus
related_terms: strong-force
related_terms: electric-force
related_terms: radioactivity
related_terms: radionuclide
related_terms: isotope
quick_facts: Stable: the nucleus stays as it is
quick_facts: Unstable: the nucleus will change and give off radiation
quick_facts: It depends on the mix of protons and neutrons
quick_facts: Carbon-12 and carbon-13 are stable, but carbon-14 is not
quick_facts: Most atoms around us are stable

sources:
    {"organization":"OpenStax","title":"University Physics Volume 3: Nuclear Binding Energy","url":"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/10-2-nuclear-binding-energy"}

definition:
    Whether a nucleus stays as it is or will change one day. A nucleus is stable when the strong force that holds it together balances the electric push between its protons. Stability depends on the mix of protons and neutrons: each element has only a few neutron counts that give a stable nucleus. Most atoms around us are stable. The others are unstable, which means radioactive.

debunking_snippet:
    “More neutrons always make a nucleus safer.” Too many neutrons can unbalance a nucleus too, as in carbon-14.
